package mop;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

import br.unb.cic.mop.eh.*;

/**
 * RandomStringPasswordSpec
 *
 * The set's only dataflow bridge, and the only specification of the set that
 * translates no CrySL rule: the oracle's 49 rules state no RandomStringPassword
 * rule, and the only rule of that family is SecureRandom.crysl. It existed to carry
 * `randomized` predicate across two JDK conversions, Object -> String -> char[],
 * so that PBEKeySpecSpec.c1's read of that predicate over a `char[]` -- the only
 * such read in the set, and one nothing else can satisfy, since every other
 * producer yields a byte[], an int or the generator itself -- could be met.
 *
 * The four predicate sites that made up the bridge are gone: the two guard reads
 * and the two writes they governed. What decided it is that the bridge does not
 * carry what it stamps, measured over each of the three source types the set can
 * hand it (researcher, 2026-08-21):
 *
 *   byte[]        String.valueOf calls Object.toString, so the string is the
 *                 identity string -- measured, "[B@726f3b58" -- and holds a heap
 *                 identity hash, not one bit of the array it names.
 *   SecureRandom  the generator itself carries `randomized`, and converts to the
 *                 constant "SecureRandom", the same text in every program.
 *   Integer       the only faithful conversion, and the one that does not
 *                 survive: the predicate store keys a bound object by identity,
 *                 and the box made at the write and the box made at the read are
 *                 the same object only inside the Integer cache (-128..127) --
 *                 where, for `SecureRandom.nextInt(int)`, the marked value is the
 *                 bound argument and not the random result.
 *
 * So the two source types that propagate carry no randomness and the one that
 * carries randomness does not propagate. On the frozen seed, where both ends of
 * the bridge sat on the same substrate, the consequence is a false negative that
 * was measured rather than argued: a PBEKeySpec built from the char[] of
 * "[B@6ae40994" is accepted as having a randomised password and draws nothing.
 * Recording the pair as `propagation` would have put the set's name on a fact the
 * conversion does not support.
 *
 * Deleting them moves no observable behaviour. The bridge was already inert in
 * this set: its reads were still on the old substrate while its producers moved
 * to the new one, so nothing had reached it since. Its sole consumer keeps
 * reporting exactly what it reported before -- and that consumer stands behind no
 * clause either: PBEKeySpec.crysl:29 REQUIRES `randomized[salt]`, and the clauses
 * it states about the password are `neverTypeOf[password, java.lang.String]` and
 * `notHardCoded[password]` (:25-26). That divergence is recorded against
 * PBEKeySpecSpec, where
 * it lives, and is not repaired from here.
 *
 * The two events stay, and so does the automaton. Deleting them would make the
 * calls unmodelled rather than unmarked -- the disposition `CipherSpec.wkb1` and
 * `SecureRandomSpec.ints` already received. The empty `@match` stays for a reason
 * that is not style: the JavaMOP grammar requires at least one handler after the
 * `ere` (RVParser.propertyHandler), and a file without one does not parse, so an
 * empty handler is the only way to state an automaton with nothing to report.
 */
RandomStringPasswordSpec(String str) {

    event vo after(Object obj) returning(String s):
       call(public static String String.valueOf(Object)) &&
       args(obj) {
    }

    event gb after(String s) returning(char[] chars):
      call(public char[] String.toCharArray()) &&
      target(s) {
    }

    ere : vo gb

    @match {

    }
}
